From 0a67f1952013f8012524d8365a9d6e207a777cf3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 11:16:41 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Ordered assembly of the stiffness matrices computed by
 kernels

The stiffness matrices computed by kernels are added to the sparse matrix
of the tangent operator by atomic additions, whose order depends on the
scheduling of the threads on a GPU: the values differ in the last digits
from one assembly to the next, and so do the results of a computation.
The accelerations of the fixed point solver then stagnate in rare runs
(FixedPointSolverTest failed about once in twenty runs on the GPU).

The new `TangentOperatorAssembly` parameter (default value given by the
`MFEM_MGIS_TANGENT_OPERATOR_ASSEMBLY` environment variable) selects
`Atomic` (default) or `Ordered`. In the ordered assembly, the stiffness
matrices of all the elements are stored element by element, then added by
one thread per row of the matrix (`batch::addStiffnessMatricesByRows`), in
the order of the rows of the stiffness matrices of the elements
contributing to each row (`batch::ElementsRows`, computed once): the
results are the same at each run, on a GPU too, at the cost of the storage
of the stiffness matrices of the elements. This also applies to the
elastic operator of the fixed point solver and to the operators of the
FBar formulation and of the regularization of Faltus et al.

Tests: assembly row by row compared to the atomic additions and the same
at each assembly, invalid rows, sizes and patterns refused
(GradientsKernelsTest); ordered assembly of the integrator in the three
layouts of the rows; problems with two materials, springs and pressure,
FBar and Faltus et al., identical bit for bit at each run
(BatchIntegrationTest); FixedPointSolverTest with kernels and the ordered
assembly on the CPU and on the GPU; invalid value refused.
